However, maybe the expected answer is just "There are 2^8 = 256 such rational numbers." The problem says find all, but if a contest asks "Find all rational numbers ..." maybe they might accept description. The final can include both description and count. Box maybe:
\boxed{\left\{\frac{\prod_{r\in S} r^{v_r(25!)}}{\prod_{r\notin S} r^{v_r(25!)}} : S\subseteq\{2,3,5,7,11,13,17,19,23\},\ \prod_{r\in S} r^{v_r(25!)}<\sqrt{25!}\right\}}
There are 256 such numbers.

Could we maybe explicitly list all possible numerator factors as "any divisor of 25! that contains either the full power or none of each prime"? That's simpler:
\boxed{\left\{\frac{d}{25!/d}: d\mid 25!,\ \gcd(d,25!/d)=1,\ d<25!/d\right\}}
But this includes condition gcd equivalent to full powers; maybe less explicit. Better to list prime powers.

All such numbers are
\boxed{\left\{\frac{\prod_{r\in S} r^{v_r(25!)}}{\prod_{r\notin S} r^{v_r(25!)}} \;:\; S\subseteq\{2,3,5,7,11,13,17,19,23\},\ \prod_{r\in S} r^{v_r(25!)}<\sqrt{25!}\right\}.}
or explicitly:
\boxed{\left\{\frac{\prod_{r\in S} r^{e_r}}{25!/\prod_{r\in S} r^{e_r}}: S\subseteq\{2,3,5,7,11,13,17,19,23\},\ \prod_{r\in S} r^{e_r}<25!/\prod_{r\in S} r^{e_r}\right\}}
where e_2=22,... etc.

Let's maybe include a note: The set-builder condition "p<q" is not a separate arbitrary check? It selects half. Maybe we can write: For every choice of S, if the resulting p>q, replace p by q? But p/q then maybe not product? If swap, product same but p<q. The set of all can be described as: For each unordered pair {S,S^c}, let a=min(∏_{S},∏_{S^c}), b=max(...); fraction a/b. That may be more constructive and avoids inequality with sqrt. Box:
\boxed{\left\{\frac{\min(A_S,A_{S^c})}{\max(A_S,A_{S^c})}: S\subseteq\{2,3,...,23\}\right\}, A_S=\prod_{r\in S} r^{e_r}.}
But set over all S duplicates each fraction twice; to avoid duplicates need quotient by complement or condition. The inequality avoids duplicates. The min/max with S over all subsets duplicates (each fraction twice) but set automatically eliminates duplicates. Could be okay. But better condition S with A_S < A_{S^c}.

Need ensure final answer's boxed set is not too wide. Could use aligned:
\boxed{
\left\{
\frac{\prod_{r\in S} r^{v_r(25!)}}{\prod_{r\notin S} r^{v_r(25!)}}
\;middle;
S\subseteq\{2,3,5,7,11,13,17,19,23\},\ 
\prod_{r\in S} r^{v_r(25!)}<\sqrt{25!}
\right\}
}

Maybe add "where v_2=22,...".

Could include count inside box:
\boxed{\text{The 256 fractions }\frac{a}{25!/a}\text{ where }a=\prod_{r\in S}r^{v_r(25!)}, S\subseteq\{2,3,5,7,11,13,17,19,23\}, a<25!/a.}

Final answer can be:
\boxed{\left\{\frac{A}{25!/A}: A=\prod_{x\in B}x \text{ for some }B\subseteq\{2^{22},3^{10},5^6,7^3,11^2,13,17,19,23\},\ A<25!/A\right\}.}
There are 256.

Let's write:
"Thus the complete set is
\boxed{...}
and its cardinality is \boxed{256}."
But user said final answer within boxed. Multiple boxes okay? Maybe one box with both. We can do one box at end with set and note count. Or separate: final set box and count sentence. To be safe, one final box containing set and count:
\boxed{\left\{ ... \right\}; \text{ there are }256\text{ such rational numbers}.}

Need be careful with semicolon in box. Good.

Let's formulate final box:
\boxed{
\left\{
\frac{A}{25!/A}
\;middle;
A=\prod_{x\in B}x,\ B\subseteq\{2^{22},3^{10},5^6,7^3,11^2,13,17,19,23\},\ A<25!/A
\right\}
\quad(\text{256 fractions}).
}
